Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  23 REMOVED_MONTH 94 ACCESSIONED ON  23 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 15 38 CLINICAL DATA  History of ovarian cancer status post multiple recurrences  the patient has been in remission but now presents with a left breast mass  Rule out breast carcinoma versus metastatic ovarian carcinoma                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INFILTRATING CARCINOMA  EXTENDING TO RESECTION MARGIN   SEE NOTE  NOTE  The histology of the neoplasm is more consistent with primary ductal carcinoma of the breast than it is with metastatic carcinoma of the ovary  Most of the tumor is infiltrating  but some ducts contain in situ carcinoma  a feature supportive of the tumor being primary in the breast  A GCDFP 15 immunostain  which if positive  is supportive evidence for a primary breast carcinoma  is only focally positive in this case  Dr  Koerner has seen the H and E slides and also favors breast as the primary site for the carcinoma  Diagnosis by  G  Richard Dickersin  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 14 46                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  04 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 10 26 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Left breast  is a 8 x REMOVED_DATE x 3cm piece of fibroadipose tissue  containing an overlying  unremarkable skin ellipse measuring REMOVED_DATE x 1 7cm  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm  pink tan  variegated  focally necrotic and hemorrhagic nodule with irregular borders  The nodule is present at multiple inked resection margins and is located more 2cm from the overlying skin  A representative section is frozen as frozen section  1  and saved frozen  Representative sections of the remaining specimen are submitted as follows  Section Code  A D  tumor  E F  tumor at margin  G  skin  H I  representative breast parenchyma  Resident  STAMATINA KAPTAIN  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1  BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  EDWIN S  MONUKI  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Charles Mason Ferguson  M D 